</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Using Large Language Models (LLMs) in real-world applications presents significant challenges, particularly in balancing computational efficiency and performance. Optimizing acceleration after the fine-tuning phase and during inference is crucial for building an efficient architecture. This paper introduces Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration, a novel training and inference method that dynamically adjusts hardware usage during inference without requiring additional fine-tuning. The proposed approach is applied to newly developed models and evaluated across multiple classification and text generation tasks. Experimental results demonstrate that the method enables a wide range of acceleration in a zero-shot manner and achieves up to a 11× speedup compared to the baseline.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, numerous large language models (LLMs) have emerged, with performance generally improving as model size increases. However, the development and deployment of these large-scale models are often limited to major corporations and research institutions due to the substantial demands on computational resources and hardware. This limits their practicality in real-world applications such as industrial safety systems and time-sensitive data monitoring platforms, where computational efficiency is critical.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training state-of-the-art LLMs requires vast amounts of data, energy, and hardware. For instance, LLaMA 3 by Meta AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite> required 39.3 million GPU hours on H100-80GB hardware, consuming 27.5 GWh of electricity and emitting an estimated 11,390 tons of <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CO_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CO_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Although Meta offsets this with renewable energy and net-zero emissions, the resource intensity remains a significant barrier to broader adoption.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, inference latency and limited access to large datasets further complicate LLM deployment. While fine-tuning can help adapt models to specific tasks, computational constraints continue to hinder their use in practical settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This work proposes a novel architecture and training policy that introduces a dynamic tuning parameter, enabling zero-shot adjustment of model acceleration. This approach promotes efficient resource utilization and reduced inference latency, particularly in services with fluctuating workloads—without requiring retraining. The main contributions of this work are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A new word vector pruning architecture that stably accelerates inference time by up to 11×.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A fine-tuning policy that prepares the model for zero-shot adjustable acceleration, enabling flexible speed–accuracy trade-offs at inference time without the need for additional training or re-finetuning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed method does not alter the core architecture and can be applied alongside other acceleration techniques.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reviews related works focused on accelerating LLM inference. The methods are categorized into model compression techniques, efficient architecture designs, and dynamic inference techniques.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Model Compression Techniques</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model compression techniques are designed to accelerate inference while minimizing accuracy loss by reducing the computational complexity of neural networks. This can be achieved by compressing various components, such as weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoo et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nova et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Belcak and Wattenhofer, 2023</a>)</cite>, attention heads or channels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, layers or blocks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sajjad et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and word vector representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anagnostidis et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaegle et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kachuee and Sharifkhani, 2025</a>)</cite>. A widely used alternative is knowledge distillation, where a smaller student model learns to replicate the behavior of a larger, more complex teacher model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanh et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu and Liu, 2020</a>)</cite>. Quantization techniques further improve efficiency by lowering the numerical precision of model weights, reducing memory usage and speeding up inference without retraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Efficient Architecture Design</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixture of Experts (MoE) enables models to be pretrained with significantly lower computational costs, allowing for substantial scaling of model or dataset size while maintaining the same compute budget as a dense model. Notably, an MoE model can achieve the same quality as its dense counterpart much faster during pretraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanseviero et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Another variant of efficient architecture design is Sparse Attention Mechanisms, which optimize computation by focusing on relevant tokens rather than attending to all tokens. Techniques such as Longformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beltagy et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, BigBird <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and FlashAttention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao, 2024</a>)</cite> leverage sparse attention to enhance efficiency in processing long sequences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Dynamic Inference Techniques</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adaptive computation dynamically adjusts resources per token or layer based on task difficulty. A common strategy is the use of early exit mechanisms, which terminate computation once confident predictions are achieved. For instance, ELBERT introduces a confidence-window-based early exit mechanism that significantly improves ALBERT’s inference speed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Similarly, D-LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> employs a dynamic decision module at each transformer layer to conditionally skip or execute units, reducing computation and KV (key-value) storage by up to 45% on question answering, summarization, and math tasks, and by 50% on commonsense reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">RT-LM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> addresses uncertainty in real-time LLM inference with an uncertainty-aware resource management system that reduces latency caused by unpredictable inputs. Llumnix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> improves load balancing and priority-based scheduling by dynamically rescheduling tasks across model instances, drawing parallels to CPU context switching.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond early exit, dynamic routing approaches offer further optimization. Unlike fixed Top-K Mixture of Experts (MoE) routing, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024)</a></cite> propose a confidence-based expert selection mechanism for each input. Radial Networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dotzel et al., 2024</a>)</cite> use a trained router to perform token-level routing, enabling model scaling while reducing computational cost. BlockLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> enhances efficiency by sharing components across multiple fine-tuned LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raposo et al. (2024)</a></cite> demonstrate that transformers can dynamically allocate FLOPs per token position within a fixed compute budget by limiting the number of tokens processed in self-attention and MLP layers. COPAL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malla et al., 2024</a>)</cite> achieves efficient pruning of LLMs through sensitivity analysis, avoiding expensive retraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Additional strategies include dynamic deep neural networks (DNNs), which scale channels and layers in real time to meet varying resource demands <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. DODO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> replaces fixed vector representations with a dynamic number of hidden states, reducing self-attention costs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kachuee and Sharifkhani (2025)</a></cite> propose identifying and removing less important word vectors using the Attention Context Contribution (ACC) metric, enabling inference-time latency adjustment without additional training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Key challenges in this area include maintaining stable inference performance, optimizing acceleration post-training, and supporting a broad and reliable range of acceleration levels.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Proposed Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the proposed method, starting with an overview of LLM architectures, highlighting key similarities and differences. It then describes the attention layer functionality and the concept of word vector contribution within the attention mechanism. The proposed word vector pruning strategy is introduced, detailing its methodology and benefits. Finally, the section concludes with an explanation of the zero-shot adjustable acceleration approach.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>LLMs Architectures Review</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the mainstream development of Large Language Models (LLMs) in recent years has primarily focused on decoder-only architectures. Following this trend, several research centers have introduced multiple LLM families, including LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, Gemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, and GPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The review of various LLM architectures, including LLaMA 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, LLaMA 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Gemma 1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, Gemma 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, Mistral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, reveals significant architectural similarities. These include overall structure, activation functions, residual branches, and attention layer designs. A comparative summary is provided in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 LLMs Architectures Review ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
