<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>
A comparative summary of the architectures of GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, Gemma 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, Mistral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and LlaMA 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 LLMs Architectures Review ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, GPT-2, Gemma 2, Mistral, and LLaMA 3 exhibit significant structural similarities in their architectures. This analysis suggests that developing a universal acceleration platform compatible with a broad range of Transformer-based architectures is both feasible and practical.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Word Vector Context Contribution</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Transformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. (2017)</a></cite> and its successors have achieved remarkable success across various Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks. At its core, the attention layer serves as the most critical component of the model. This layer is primarily designed in two forms: bidirectional attention, which is commonly used in encoder architectures for text classification tasks, and causal attention, which is predominantly employed in decoder architectures for text generation tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The primary distinction between causal attention and bidirectional attention lies in how tokens interact within a Transformer model during processing. Causal attention restricts each token to attending only to previous tokens in the sequence, making it particularly suitable for autoregressive text generation. In contrast, bidirectional attention allows tokens to attend to both preceding and succeeding tokens, which is essential for tasks such as text classification and masked language modeling. The mathematical formulation of the attention layer is presented in the following equation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Att(Q,K,V)=softmax(\frac{QK^{T}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}+M)V" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Att(Q,K,V)=softmax(\frac{QK^{T}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}+M)V</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the attention mechanism, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> (queries), <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (keys), and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> (values) are matrices derived from the input context. The key dimension, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, is used for scaling, where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> prevents large dot-product values from dominating the softmax function. The attention mask <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> plays a crucial role in differentiating between causal and bidirectional attention: in causal attention, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a lower triangular matrix with <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> in future positions to prevent tokens from attending to future inputs, whereas in bidirectional attention, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing all tokens to attend to each other.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The softmax function normalizes the attention scores, referred to as the <em id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">soft-score</em>. The <em id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">soft-score</em> is a square matrix with dimensions corresponding to the input sequence length. By computing the dot product between the <em id="S3.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">soft-score</em> and the values matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, the contribution of each row in the values matrix—representing a single word vector—to the layer output is determined.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Dynamic Resource Attention</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in the previous section, the <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">soft-score</em> determines each word vector’s contribution to the layer output. We assume that word vectors with lower contributions can be eliminated in subsequent layers when inference acceleration is needed. However, key bottlenecks in previous works include maintaining stable inference performance, enabling efficient zero-shot acceleration, and ensuring a broad range of stable acceleration. Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.3 Dynamic Resource Attention ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the proposed dynamic resource attention architecture.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S3.F1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.F1.2.1" class="ltx_text"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.01190v1/fig1.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:286/592;" width="286" height="592"></object></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The proposed dynamic resource attention</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.3 Dynamic Resource Attention ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the proposed dynamic resource attention architecture eliminates word vectors with lower contributions to the attention layer output based on the required inference acceleration, which is controlled by the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> hyperparameter. The number of remaining word vectors in layer <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the following equation:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{l}=Max(\alpha N_{l-1},\tau_{l})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{l}=Max(\alpha N_{l-1},\tau_{l})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the number of remaining word vectors at layer <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of remaining word vectors from the previous layer, and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the elimination rate hyperparameter, which is a floating-point value between 0 and 1. Decreasing the value of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> reduces the number of preserved tokens in each layer. For example, setting <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.3</annotation></semantics></math> means that in each layer of the Transformer architecture, only 30% of the word vectors are preserved, while 70% are eliminated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The hyperparameter <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> plays a crucial role in the performance of the proposed method. It serves as a threshold parameter that regulates the pruning process and sets a minimum limit on the number of preserved word vectors. This parameter prevents a scenario where only a single token remains in the attention layer, which would render the model unstable and unusable. The clipping mechanism effectively enhances the stability of fine-tuning while also improving the inference performance of the accelerated model at high pruning rates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Optimizing acceleration after fine-tuning and during inference is crucial for an efficient architecture, referred to as zero-shot acceleration. Fine-tuning in LLMs is a progressive process where the model adapts to the downstream task. Studies show that a fixed acceleration rate during fine-tuning causes a performance drop when deviated during zero-shot acceleration. However, if the acceleration rate is scheduled and varied during fine-tuning, the model becomes biased toward the last observed rates due to the gradual adaptation process. In the proposed method, to overcome the aforementioned problem, the acceleration rate is sampled from a uniform distribution for each batch of fine-tuning samples. The following equation describes the acceleration rate (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) at each fine-tuning step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=U(0.02,1.00)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.02</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.00</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=U(0.02,1.00)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By employing the described distribution during the fine-tuning phase, the model experiences a dynamic acceleration rate, ranging from 2% to 100% token preservation per layer. This approach enhances the model’s resilience to acceleration rate variations during the zero-shot acceleration phase.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 LLMs Architectures Review ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, recent large language models (LLMs) share significant architectural similarities. To comprehensively evaluate the proposed method, we select GPT-2 as a legacy model, representing earlier generations of LLMs, and LLaMA 3 and Gemma 2 as newly proposed, state-of-the-art models. The performance of the Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration method is assessed across text classification, text generation, and instruction tuning tasks, using multiple benchmarks and datasets.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed method is evaluated on natural language understanding tasks using the IMDB dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maas et al. (2011)</a></cite> for sentiment analysis and the GLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2018)</a></cite>. GLUE includes a variety of tasks, such as single-sentence classification (SST-2), sentence similarity (MRPC, QQP), question answering (QNLI), and natural language inference (RTE, MNLI-M, MNLI-MM).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the method’s effectiveness on text generation tasks, we use several datasets. WikiText-103 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merity et al. (2016)</a></cite>, Penn Treebank (PTB) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marcus et al. (1993)</a></cite>, and One Billion Word (1BW) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chelba et al. (2014)</a></cite> serve as primary benchmarks for language modeling. We also include the LAMBADA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paperno et al. (2016)</a></cite>, which requires long-range context understanding to predict the final word in narrative passages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, to evaluate the approach on instruction-tuning tasks, we use the Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU) benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2020)</a></cite>. MMLU consists of exam-style questions spanning 57 diverse subjects, including mathematics, history, law, and medicine.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Models Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the GPT-2, LLaMA 3, and Gemma 2 models, the attention layer in each decoder is modified according to the proposed architecture described in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Dynamic Resource Attention ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. The hyperparameter <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> controls the word-vector elimination rate, enabling adjustable acceleration. Evaluation is conducted using the following hyperparameters: a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\text{\times}{10}^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\text{\times}{10}^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, a batch size of 8, 5 training epochs, a dropout rate of 0.1, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values ranging from 0.02 to 1.00 for GPT-2, and from 0.50 to 1.00 for LLaMA 3 and Gemma 2 (see Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>). Additionally, the minimum number of preserved word vectors (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>) is fixed at 5 to ensure stability, as discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Dynamic Resource Attention ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. These settings define the experimental configuration used to evaluate the proposed method’s impact on GPT-2, LLaMA 3, and Gemma 2.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study utilizes pre-trained GPT-2 and LLaMA 3 and Gemma 2 models from the Hugging Face<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/</a></span></span></span> library, implemented in PyTorch<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://pytorch.org/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://pytorch.org/</a></span></span></span> and executed on an NVIDIA Tesla T4 GPU. Datasets are sourced from the Hugging Face Datasets library, while GLUE results are validated using the official GLUE evaluation service<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://gluebenchmark.com/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://gluebenchmark.com/</a></span></span></span>, ensuring consistency and reliability in performance evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To efficiently fine-tune LLaMA 3 and Gemma 2, the fine-tuning policy proposed in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is accelerated using the BitsAndBytes library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al. (2023)</a></cite>, which enables efficient deployment of large language models through k-bit quantization. Additional acceleration is achieved through Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2021)</a></cite>. Together, these techniques substantially reduce the hardware resource requirements associated with the fine-tuning process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.4 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the results of the proposed Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration method applied to GPT-2 on the IMDB dataset and the GLUE benchmark. Reducing the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> value leads to increased inference speed, achieving up to an 11× speedup on the IMDB dataset and an average latency reduction of approximately 9×. These findings confirm the effectiveness of the fine-tuning policy and demonstrate that inference latency can be dynamically adjusted without additional fine-tuning. Due to constraints related to the test sets, results for GLUE are reported using the validation set.</p>
</div>
