<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>
Experimental results of the proposed Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration method for GPT-2 on the IMDB dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maas et al., 2011</a>)</cite> and the GLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. Performance is measured using F1-score for MRPC and QQP, and accuracy for other tasks. Bold values highlight the maximum speed–accuracy trade-off where the model remains functional.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.4 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the performance of the proposed method for GPT-2 across three aspects: (A) Zero-Shot Acceleration results, demonstrating its stability and effectiveness; (B) the ratio of preserved word vectors, highlighting significant memory savings; and (C) the selected range of the elimination rate hyperparameter (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating enhanced effectiveness on datasets with longer sequence lengths.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S4.F2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F2.2.1" class="ltx_text"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.01190v1/fig2.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:419/150;" width="419" height="150"></object></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
Visualization of GPT-2 performance: (A) zero-shot acceleration results, (B) preserved word vector ratio, and (C) elimination rate hyperparameter (<math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) across a 1×–11× acceleration range. Results are reported using F1-score for MRPC and QQP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and accuracy for other tasks.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.4 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents perplexity (PPL) results for GPT-2 on WikiText-103, LAMBADA, PTB, and 1BW datasets. The proposed method demonstrates effectiveness across various acceleration settings, achieving up to a 5× improvement in Time-to-First-Token (TTFT). These results suggest that the input sentence context is well preserved, thereby validating the word vector pruning strategy.</p>
</div>
