<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>
Perplexity (PPL) experimental results for the GPT-2 model on the WikiText-103 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merity et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, LAMBADA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paperno et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, PTB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marcus et al., 1993</a>)</cite>, and 1BW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chelba et al., 2014</a>)</cite> datasets. Note that the reported result for 1BW is evaluated on 0.5% of the test set.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the performance of the proposed method on LLaMA 3 and Gemma 2, evaluated using the MMLU benchmark. The method effectively reduces Multiply-Accumulate Operations (MACs) during inference while maintaining strong accuracy, demonstrating its applicability to both legacy and modern models. Given that both models are quantized and executed on GPUs, reporting MACs offers a more reliable measure of performance than Time-to-First-Token (TTFT), due to the characteristics of GPU acceleration.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
