<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>
Zero-shot acceleration results showing the reduction in Multiply-Accumulate Operations (MACs) achieved by the proposed approach on LLaMA 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al. (2024)</a></cite> and Gemma 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, evaluated using the MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2020)</a></cite> instruction tuning dataset.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the results in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Efficient Large Language Models with Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the proposed fine-tuning policy demonstrates effective performance when used in conjunction with the LoRA-based fine-tuning acceleration method. Furthermore, the method is compatible with 4-bit quantized models, offering both improved acceleration and performance. These results confirm that the proposed approach does not alter the core model architecture and can be seamlessly integrated with other acceleration techniques.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper reviews recent advancements in large language model (LLM) architectures and introduces a novel acceleration framework. The proposed fine-tuning policy ensures model stability across a wide range of zero-shot acceleration settings. Termed Zero-Shot Adjustable Acceleration, this approach enables dynamic adjustment of inference speed without the need for additional fine-tuning. Experimental results on GPT-2, LLaMA 3, and Gemma 2 demonstrate that the method is applicable to both legacy and state-of-the-art LLMs, achieving up to 11× acceleration while maintaining performance. These findings support the notion that, although the full network is essential during initial training, selective pruning can significantly improve efficiency with minimal performance degradation.</p>
</div>
</section>
